[#:seon.task{:name "a-throwing-step-holds-its-message-and-parks",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (e3_failure E3.1): a throwing step loses its message (flow/impl.clj proc catch), keeps its old state and goes on; the only record is a sliding(100) error chan. With the hold pattern (e3 `held`, ~25 lines) the proc keeps the message, emits a :failed fact with the cause, and narrows ::flow/input-filter to nothing, so its producers park by back-pressure and its consumers park on empty inputs (E3.2: no crash, no timeout, nothing dropped). On the load effect's one broadcast after a reload (a ::reloaded signal, flow's own signal-select) it retries the held message through the Var; E3.3: the fix ran the held message and the 8 queued behind it, in order, with no other intervention. Correct: seon.step/held (or the same in flow's proc loop), used by every root proc; my.fx's load owner casts ::reloaded with the vars it changed. A request a held proc took is answered when it completes, never with a fake fallback (overlaps `seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took`).",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{seon.step-test/a-reload-retries-the-held-message
               seon.step-test/a-throw-holds-the-message-and-records-the-cause}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-edge-asks-one-way"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the one-offs survey (notes/one-offs-2026-10-23.md, item 8; owner, 2026-10-23: src resists growth so agents reuse and improve the core parts rather than making one-offs, DECISIONS). What existed: an edge's request to a proc written three times: seon.root/call! (a promise put with the request, a closed :ext answered root stopped), seon.web's ask! (the same, but a closed channel left its HTTP thread waiting forever) and seon.root/stop!'s :shutdown (a closed :ext waited out both of stop!'s bounds, about 18 s, on a promise nothing would deliver). Correct: seon.step/ask-edge, beside the asker's side every proc shares (pend, ask, answered), puts the request with a fresh promise and answers a closed channel at once with the caller's closed value; the three call it. A law: no src form outside seon.step puts a request carrying :p on a channel (read as data from the compiler's facts)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.step-test/an-edge-asks-one-way seon.main-test/an-edge-asks-one-way}}]
